<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hiraizumi</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hiraizumi is a town in Iwate prefecture in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honshu">Honshu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. In the 12th century it was the seat of the Northern Fujiwara, who ruled Mutsu and Dewa and, with gold from the region, made it a rich cultural centre rivalling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minamoto-no-yoritomo">Minamoto no Yoritomo</a> destroyed the family in 1189. Its temples and gardens, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chuson-ji">Chūson-ji</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/motsu-ji">Mōtsū-ji</a>, became a World Heritage Site in 2011.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hiraizumi.jpg" alt="Hiraizumi" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 663highland · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:230728_Chusonji_Hiraizumi_Iwate_pref_Japan01s3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
